an>Payment Commitments</span></div></li><li class="pillar"><span class="pillar__num">03</span><h3>Collect more of what you already earned</h3><p>Most &#x27;missing&#x27; money was billed and never followed. LexCollect shows what&#x27;s late, who owes it and what each account is worth, so the biggest dollars are worked first.</p><div class="pillar__mods"><span>Collections monthly view</span><span>Aging breakdown</span><span>Revenue forecast</span><span>Collection flow</span></div></li><li class="pillar"><span class="pillar__num">04</span><h3>Recover hard debt without burning the relationship</h3><p>Ninety-day-plus balances get a sequence, not a shouting match: reconciled balance first, a clear message, a plan the client can keep, and a record of every promise.</p><div class="pillar__mods"><span>Hard-debt sequences</span><span>Hardship requests</span><span>Card Recovery</span><span>Promise tracking</span></div></li><li class="pillar"><span class="pillar__num">05</span><h3>Automation that runs like a finance department</h3><p>Continuous reconciliation, duplicate and orphan cleanup, migration between systems and payment booking that can&#x27;t double-count. The work an elite accounting team would do by hand, done every day.</p><div class="pillar__mods"><span>Continuous reconciliation</span><span>Migration reconciliation</span><span>Unmatched payment review</span><span>Idempotent booking</span></div></li><li class="pillar"><span class="pillar__num">06</span><h3>Hold the team accountable</h3><p>Every outreach, outcome, dollar and commission by team member, by day. Scorecards, contact rates and a daily activity log that doesn&#x27;t depend on memory.</p><div class="pillar__mods"><span>Team Performance</span><span>Daily Activity Log</span><span>Outcome Distribution</span><span>Collections KPI</span></div></li><li class="pillar"><span class="pillar__num">07</span><h3>Unique escalations and internal connection</h3><p>Billing, service teams, intake and management look at the same client, the same contract status and the same escalation. Hand-offs carry a queue, a priority and an owner instead of an email chain.</p><div class="pillar__mods"><span>Escalation Management</span><span>Department inboxes</span><span>Hand-off queues</span><span>Deadline watch</span></div></li><li class="pillar"><span class="pillar__num">08</span><h3>Know which products make the money, and when</h3><p>Billing by product, contract lifecycle and progression, and the service pipeline by stage. See which products generate cash and where in the life of an engagement it arrives.</p><div class="pillar__mods"><span>Billing by Product</span><span>Contract Lifecycle</span><span>Service Pipeline</span><span>Product Mix</span></div></li><li class="pillar"><span class="pillar__num">09</span><h3>Works with the CRM you already have</h3><p>Compatible with any CRM or practice system that exports or has an API connection. Intake and consult tracking, deals validated against real payments, and client data joined to money so the CRM and the books finally agree.</p><div class="pillar__mods"><span>Any CRM with export or API</span><span>HubSpot sync</span><span>Consult funnel</span><span>Client 360</span></div></li></ol><div class="actions"><a class="btn btn--ghost" href="<?php echo esc_url( lexcollect_page_url( 'platform' ) ); ?>">Explore the platform <span class="arrow" aria-hidden="true">→</span></a></div></div></section><section class="section section--deep section--rule" id="management"><div class="container"><div class="split split--center"><div><p class="eyebrow">AR Management</p><h2>A receivables number the partners can trust, every month.</h2><p class="lead">Most firms have four versions of &quot;what we&#x27;re owed&quot;: the client system, the processor, the books and a spreadsheet. LexCollect reconciles them into one, then explains every dollar of difference.</p><p><strong>Reconciled, not exported.</strong> Payments are traced from the client system to the bank deposit, including payments made by someone other than the client.<br/><strong>Certified snapshots.</strong> AR is captured monthly, so trends are real history, not a re-run of today&#x27;s data.<br/><strong>Nothing dropped.</strong> A payment that can&#x27;t be tied to a client is counted and queued for a person, never left in a suspense account.</p><p class="muted">At the first firm we deployed in, the reconciled book landed within 5% of the firm&#x27;s own ground truth, and the remaining gap was a named backlog with an owner. <a href="<?php echo esc_url( lexcollect_page_url( 'results' ) ); ?>">Read how.</a></p></div><div class="monitor" aria-label="Example AR management checks"><div class="monitor__head"><span><b>Management checks</b></span><span>Example</span></div><ul><li><span class="name">ar.snapshot.monthly</span><span class="state">Certified</span><span class="meta">Captured on the 1st · trend over 12 months</span></li><li><span class="name">reconcile.processor_to_contracts</span><span class="state">Healthy</span><span class="meta">2,013 matched · 759 queued for review</span></li><li class="is-flagged"><span class="name">contracts.status.paid_without_payments</span><span class="state">Flagged</span><span class="meta">428 contracts marked Paid with ~4% collected · reclassified, audited, reversible</span></li><li><span class="name">clients.duplicates_and_orphans</span><span class="state">Healthy</span><span class="meta">561 orphan contracts re-linked · 89 duplicates merged</span></li><li><span class="name">payments.idempotency</span><span class="state">Healthy</span><span class="meta">Webhook retries can&#x27;t book a payment twice</span></li></ul><div class="monitor__foot">Every check has an owner. A green status means the job really ran.</div></div></div></div></section><section class="section section--rule" id="connect"><div class="container"><div class="section-head"><p class="eyebrow">Connect</p><h2>From &quot;they stopped paying&quot; to a message, a plan and a payment.</h2><p class="lead">The connection between a firm and its clients is the part most AR tools skip. LexCollect runs it as a loop with a record at every step, so a partner can see what happened on any account without asking.</p></div><ol class="steps"><li class="step"><span class="step__num">01</span><h3>Queue</h3><p>Late accounts ranked by balance, aging and last contact, built from reconciled balances. Consult-only and junk records are kept out by default.</p></li><li class="step"><span class="step__num">02</span><h3>Connect</h3><p>Automated email and SMS to the right client list: a missed installment, a failed card, a balance due. Clear, on brand, and logged. A person steps in only when the client needs one.</p></li><li class="step"><span class="step__num">03</span><h3>Commit</h3><p>Promises to pay get a date and an amount, then get followed up automatically. Missed installments surface while they&#x27;re small.</p></li><li class="step step--highlight"><span class="step__num">04</span><h3>Escalate or close</h3><p>Hardship, service questions and disputes go to the right queue with a priority. Paid accounts drop off. Nothing sits in limbo.</p></li></ol><div class="split" style="margin-top:var(--sp-60)"><div class="card"><span class="kicker">Trust, regained</span><h3>Nobody gets a collections message for money they already paid</h3><p>Balances are reconciled before any message goes out, including payments made by a relative or employer. That alone changes how clients feel about the firm.</p></div><div class="card"><span class="kicker">Willing payers, separated</span><h3>Failed cards are not delinquency</h3><p>At one firm, LexCollect found $928K across 228 clients who wanted to pay but whose auto-draft had failed. They got an &quot;update your card&quot; message, not a collections call, and the relationship stayed intact.</p></div></div></div></section><section class="section section--light section--rule" id="results"><div class="container"><div class="section-head"><p class="eyebrow">Results</p><h2>What it did inside a working firm.</h2><p class="lead">LexCollect was built in the finance department of a high-volume firm running MyCase, Filevine, LawPay, HubSpot and QuickBooks. These are the numbers from that deployment.</p></div><div class="wins"><div class="win"><span class="win__label">Reconciliation</span><span class="win__num">$21.4M</span><p class="win__text">Receivables book reconciled to the firm&#x27;s ground truth, with the remaining gap explained line by line.</p></div><div class="win"><span class="win__label">Migration</span><span class="win__num">$3.5M</span><p class="win__text">Contract balances re-linked to the right clients during a mass financial migration between two incompatible CRMs.</p></div><div class="win"><span class="win__label">Connection</span><span class="win__num">228</span><p class="win__text">Willing payers reconnected with one message instead of a collections call. $928K of balances kept out of hard debt.</p></div><div class="win"><span class="win__label">Escalations</span><span class="win__num">7<small>queues</small></span><p class="win__text">Service teams, compliance, customer care, management, sales, billing ops and more, each with an inbox.</p></div></div><div class="actions"><a class="btn btn--ghost" href="<?php echo esc_url( lexcollect_page_url( 'results' ) ); ?>">Read the full results <span class="arrow" aria-hidden="true">→</span></a></div></div></section><section class="section section--rule" id="adapts"><div class="container"><div class="split split--wide-right"><div><p class="eyebrow">Suited to your firm</p><h2>An accounting department&#x27;s judgment, shaped to how your firm works.</h2><p class="lead">Roles, rosters, queues, message sequences, product lines and integrations are configuration, not code. Your team appears automatically. Your escalation targets are your departments. Your clients hear from your firm, in your voice.</p><p class="muted">If your firm bills clients, runs